    "questionNumber": 7,
    "section": "Section 2 - Social Monologue",
    "audioText": "(Ranger): Good afternoon and a cordial welcome to the High Peaks Nature Reserve Visitor Centre. My name is Thomas, and I'd like to orient you before you set out on our backcountry alpine trails. \n\nFirst, regarding our trail network: the Bluebell Valley Trail is currently our most family-friendly option; it's a gentle four-kilometre paved loop that winds through our ancient oak woodlands and takes roughly an hour and a half to complete. For those seeking a more rigorous excursion, the Eagle Ridge Pass climbs over six hundred metres to our panoramic observation deck. Please be aware, however, that due to recent rockfalls caused by spring thaw, the northern section of Eagle Ridge is restricted to experienced hikers equipped with sturdy waterproof boots. \n\nAlong all trails, visitors must adhere to our 'Leave No Trace' policy. We provide zero waste bins along the wilderness tracks; whatever packaging or plastic you carry into the reserve must be packed out in your own rucksacks. \n\nIf you are interested in wildlife photography, the best viewing hours for our native red deer herd are in the late afternoon, between four thirty and six o'clock, specifically around the marshlands near Heron Lake. \n\nLastly, our interactive nature exhibition and gift shop in this building will close promptly at five thirty PM, while our main car park gates are locked automatically at seven PM sharp. Please ensure you are back at your vehicles well before dusk. Have a fantastic and safe hike!",
    "contextPassage": "[IELTS LISTENING SECTION 2: Monologue in Social Context]\nListen to the nature reserve ranger giving an orientation briefing to visitors.\n\nAudio Transcript:\n(Ranger): Good afternoon and a cordial welcome to the High Peaks Nature Reserve Visitor Centre. My name is Thomas, and I'd like to orient you before you set out on our backcountry alpine trails. \n\nFirst, regarding our trail network: the Bluebell Valley Trail is currently our most family-friendly option; it's a gentle four-kilometre paved loop that winds through our ancient oak woodlands and takes roughly an hour and a half to complete. For those seeking a more rigorous excursion, the Eagle Ridge Pass climbs over six hundred metres to our panoramic observation deck. Please be aware, however, that due to recent rockfalls caused by spring thaw, the northern section of Eagle Ridge is restricted to experienced hikers equipped with sturdy waterproof boots. \n\nAlong all trails, visitors must adhere to our 'Leave No Trace' policy. We provide zero waste bins along the wilderness tracks; whatever packaging or plastic you carry into the reserve must be packed out in your own rucksacks. \n\nIf you are interested in wildlife photography, the best viewing hours for our native red deer herd are in the late afternoon, between four thirty and six o'clock, specifically around the marshlands near Heron Lake. \n\nLastly, our interactive nature exhibition and gift shop in this building will close promptly at five thirty PM, while our main car park gates are locked automatically at seven PM sharp. Please ensure you are back at your vehicles well before dusk. Have a fantastic and safe hike!",
