    "questionNumber": 13,
    "section": "Section 3 - Academic Discussion",
    "audioText": "(Professor Brooks): Welcome, Maya and Liam. Today we need to review the research design for your undergraduate thesis on tidal marine turbine performance. Have you finalized your data collection methodology?\n(Maya): Yes, Professor Brooks. We decided to focus our quantitative comparative analysis on the Pentland Firth estuary in northern Scotland, primarily because it features some of the highest tidal current velocities recorded in Europe, reaching over five metres per second.\n(Liam): And rather than building a physical scale model in the fluid dynamics lab, we've opted for high-resolution computational fluid dynamics (CFD) simulations using open-source hydrodynamic modeling software.\n(Professor Brooks): That's a sound choice, Liam, especially given the computational resources in our faculty cluster. But how will you validate your simulation findings against empirical baseline reality?\n(Maya): We obtained five years of anonymized historical telemetry data released by the European Marine Energy Centre in Orkney. That will allow us to cross-validate our algorithmic turbulence predictions against actual offshore sensor measurements.\n(Liam): Our primary obstacle right now is processing the raw acoustic Doppler current profiler datasets. The file formats are proprietary and require extensive Python preprocessing before we can feed them into our simulation mesh.\n(Professor Brooks): I can assist with that. Dr. Aris in the oceanography department has developed a custom open-access parser for those specific ADCP formats. I will email him this afternoon to grant you repository access.\n(Maya): That would save us at least three weeks of data cleaning! Thank you, Professor.",
